diff --git a/third_party/mdns-sd.patches/0002-s4-s6-bounded-cache.patch b/third_party/mdns-sd.patches/0002-s4-s6-bounded-cache.patch
index 5660359..ceba30a 100644
--- a/third_party/mdns-sd.patches/0002-s4-s6-bounded-cache.patch
+++ b/third_party/mdns-sd.patches/0002-s4-s6-bounded-cache.patch
@@ -1,5 +1,53 @@
+S4/S6: a bounded cache, capped TTLs, and no timer per record
+
+The daemon caches what the link sends it, and nothing bounded that:
+
+- A response is taken whole unless it carries a PTR answer for a type
+  nobody browses, so one of nothing but SRV, TXT or address records for
+  made-up names goes into the cache entire (service_daemon.rs:3095-3190).
+- The cache has no size limit, only TTL expiry, and the TTL is the
+  sender's u32: a record can be kept for 136 years.
+- Every record added or refreshed pushes two wake-up times onto a heap,
+  so a neighbour re-sending one fixed set grows it by 16 bytes a record a
+  packet (service_daemon.rs:1820-1822, 3187-3189).
+- Expiry follows PTR records only: SRV, TXT and NSEC records of
+  instances no PTR points to, and addresses of hosts no SRV names, stay
+  for good, and so do the names in `resolved`.
+- The run loop reads a socket until it is empty, so a steady stream of
+  packets keeps it from its commands, shutdown included.
+
+Now each record of a response is judged on its own: a PTR of a browsed
+type, an SRV or TXT of an instance such a PTR points to, an address of a
+host such an SRV names or that is being resolved, an NSEC of any of
+these. A name holds at most 2 SRV, TXT or NSEC records and a host 8
+addresses (a unique record at the cap takes the oldest one's place, as
+its cache flush would have); a type holds at most 64 instances, a source
+at most 4 of them, and a newcomer at a full type takes the place of the
+oldest instance of the source holding the most, if that source keeps
+more than the newcomer then has -- whoever fills the cache first cannot
+keep out a device with fewer instances. 2048 records in all. A received
+TTL is kept for at most 4500 s (RFC 6762 §10's figure). Cached records
+have no timers: the run loop sleeps no longer than the earliest deadline
+it finds among them, and the heap of other timers is capped at 4096
+(past that the loop wakes every second). Once a second at most, records
+nothing leads to are swept. At most 256 datagrams are read per socket
+per wake-up.
+
+Upstream's own unit tests pass as before (third_party/mdns-sd.patches/
+test.sh); the three that need IPv6 loopback or a second interface fail
+the same way without these patches where those are missing.
+
+Findings: Sukkula review 2026-09-25 kept[1] (remote memory exhaustion
+through the mDNS cache) and the Quick Share half of kept[6] (a full
+table admitted nobody new).
